<section class="hero hero--compact">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
    <h1>Documentation</h1>
    <p class="hero__copy">From your first target to parallel project tests and interactive debugging.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<article class="docs-content panel">
  <section id="start">
    <p class="eyebrow">In five minutes</p>
    <h2>Quick start</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Install and start Browser Testbench once on your machine.</li>
      <li>Check and complete the environment on the <a href="/setup">Overview</a> page.</li>
      <li>Install the client as a development dependency in your project.</li>
      <li>Copy a concrete ID from the <a href="/targets">Test targets</a> page.</li>
      <li>Start your application and use its URL in your test.</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>npm install --global browser-testbench
browser-testbench start</code></pre>
    <p>Run this command in each project that contains automated tests:</p>
    <pre><code>npm install --save-dev browser-testbench</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="targets">
    <p class="eyebrow">Convention over Configuration</p>
    <h2>Target-IDs</h2>
    <p>
      Desktop browsers use short IDs such as <code>chrome</code>. Mobile targets include the device and version, for
      example <code>safari-ios-iphone-17-pro-26-5</code>. IDs are generated from the detected environment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="automation">
    <p class="eyebrow">Project-controlled</p>
    <h2>Automated tests</h2>
    <p>Install the Node client in your project:</p>
    <pre><code>npm install --save-dev browser-testbench</code></pre>
    <pre><code>import { RemoteTestbench } from "browser-testbench/client";

const testbench = new RemoteTestbench();
const targets = await testbench.availableTargets(["chrome"]);

await testbench.forEachTarget(targets, { url: "http://127.0.0.1:3000" }, async browser => {
  await browser.fill("#email", "test@example.com");
  await browser.click('button[type="submit"]');
  await browser.waitForText("Welcome");
});</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="debugging">
    <p class="eyebrow">Remote control</p>
    <h2>Interactive debugging</h2>
    <p>Open a long-lived session from the command line or control it through a connected MCP client.</p>
    <pre><code>browser-testbench open --target chrome --url http://127.0.0.1:3000</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="mobile">
    <p class="eyebrow">Physical and virtual devices</p>
    <h2>Mobile devices</h2>
    <p>
      Android devices connected by USB appear automatically after USB debugging is enabled and authorized. Chrome must
      be installed on the device. Connecting or disconnecting a device updates the interface automatically, and
      localhost URLs are forwarded over USB for the session. Tap, swipe, pinch, portrait, and landscape are available
      through the Node client and MCP.
    </p>
    <pre><code>import { BrowserOrientation, PinchDirection, SwipeDirection } from "browser-testbench/client";

await browser.setOrientation(BrowserOrientation.Landscape);
await browser.swipe({ direction: SwipeDirection.Up });
await browser.pinch({ direction: PinchDirection.Out });</code></pre>
  </section>
</article>
